#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130064} :activity/id #uuid "24e4f3d3-57f6-51a9-afd6-0c3e9a2f8f97" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219 :topic/id #uuid "893b600f-8843-5755-aed4-951036b65cbd" :topic/math-academy-id 1999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130392 :tutorial/content "We have explored linear and affine transformations in the plane. We'll now begin to explore nonlinear transformations of plane regions. \n\nLet  $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a **transformation** on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ We write\n![](images/11/1137221ffc72c194aba941eaee899cc0a4134a92e92e86cfa65c88acd5f702ec.svg) \n\nRecall that, under this notation, \n\n- ![](images/0d/0decb326ae66f97fb75a8df874479061fb1557b0437ca8ea1e7c5ed645db4b19.svg) represents vectors belonging to the input space (i.e., the $uv$-plane), and\n\n- ![](images/09/091b7a3ddf6ad7dc35360e728c2946481b3a17f60525d09676eb469ebd518a4d.svg) represents vectors belonging to the output space (i.e., the $xy$-plane).\n\nFor example, consider the following transformation:\n\n![](images/23/239781240f2def3a5adf5331f36fa6dbdd3657ab431195c30cf3ede49d2ef3e2.svg) \n\nGeometrically, this transformation maps the rectangular region in the $uv$-plane below to a circular sector in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc80fe1271d560e9f958eba9454761d56f936567dc439853bc4181cccc3f76f.png)\n\nWe'll explore how to compute the image of a nonlinear transformation in future lessons. But for now, it is sufficient to know that \n\n- $\\mathbf T$ takes points (or vectors) from the $uv$-plane as input and maps them to points (vectors) in the $xy$-plane, and\n\n- rectangular regions in the $uv$-plane are mapped to regions in the $xy$-plane that are not necessarily parallelograms." :tutorial/id #uuid "c831da3f-8fff-5c5b-8be5-f3602a7303ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4147 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5564d0a2-de60-5f46-b603-c6061aab85f2" :step/math-academy-id 25202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130225}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129006 :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437214}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "838542bc-20ad-57ca-b114-1923593d1021" :step/math-academy-id 25204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838 :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490290}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "0954c2f4-d0e0-56be-8d88-2f803b4f5152" :step/math-academy-id 42421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130226}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130225 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130460 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n![](images/f9/f9a1c3375eed570284c4e001f119f21901f4493a0b851919b92b678a74187444.svg) \n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that  $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$ \n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n![](images/b4/b433bcafea1c84c67f2fb65ac8343a4d8401ce6d4e745f6d464b010a4258164a.svg) \n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =u{cos}^{2}⁡v+u{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0ea7366-d3d6-5645-99d8-72f7febb8d19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7668 :tutorial/title "The Jacobian Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "eadcf9f2-f662-5ba3-8bbc-b9166dfa1142" :step/math-academy-id 44550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130226 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130461 :tutorial/content "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." :tutorial/id #uuid "4dcb1ce1-bfad-524c-a89a-a589532a1067" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7672 :tutorial/title "A Review of Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "c956f456-bd29-5cf7-83bd-de9b757b718f" :step/math-academy-id 44557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130227}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130462 :tutorial/content "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones. \n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where\n![](images/d0/d0149013f12e5cdad31611d4c64489c0aaf6049707e9eed22202ae70d2d8a38a.svg) \n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$\nJ(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson). \n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$. \n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)<  0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f6a2c63-d9fc-533c-8c4b-fd822216f0f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7673 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Jacobian"} :step/id #uuid "0e6a94d3-c32f-5582-98ea-69a8605ef653" :step/math-academy-id 44558 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130230}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128860 :question/id #uuid "32f92fce-b4b9-5e7a-9fe0-255b15edf312" :question/math-academy-id "e-12417"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389216}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "4b57bfb4-8aa6-5afc-b54b-581e471d245a" :step/math-academy-id 44604 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128861 :question/id #uuid "6102567f-7035-5fa2-b12e-80a0470de4a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050992}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389209}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"} :step/id #uuid "3dc69ec2-a31b-570c-8cf5-3d69b616fa0a" :step/math-academy-id 44605}] :activity/title "The Jacobian" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129344}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406807} :activity/id #uuid "7774b093-64ee-588d-8f44-eac906b4d72f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219 :topic/id #uuid "893b600f-8843-5755-aed4-951036b65cbd" :topic/math-academy-id 1999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018385f7-7519-5922-8545-718d1e4443e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278597} {:answer/id #uuid "09f72da4-93f7-5f6e-8f5c-7adf97a2d87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278688} {:answer/id #uuid "42229dfb-92db-527f-98d4-81e464debdce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279182} {:answer/id #uuid "5806f5cb-b442-516d-a7af-f295ed7a67a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279401} {:answer/id #uuid "b63f49ff-6805-5e3d-9a84-bb5bf0cb46d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278688} :answer-field/id #uuid "30341de0-d7c9-531a-935d-0764a34a14a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a571cf4-d621-5149-95d3-896e62b990d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161875" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(0,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u-{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "68b725b2-e4f6-5584-8b86-cedb8363d41b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e468aabf-e6e4-5599-81b1-17dc6d355954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389030} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeedbec-6bdf-59e7-9b2b-b9d5e4e3ee50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389031} {:answer/id #uuid "a24ff250-c577-5a6e-8589-b7fa78f56034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389032} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf74745-74d7-5023-8d2f-494bd3a4b62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/15/154f70201a5a00737d8df61952f5600ff91a2373df07b0b54680fe84e048402c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389033} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a52325-f624-5716-886a-b507033c0141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/090bab0ac1c6356010d83a6ad54c59b53b7dd12062fb5e331a936ce3f3a1b411.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389032} :answer-field/id #uuid "391991ce-c7bf-55cb-8662-29196fd230b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4471fd6f-9a7d-5e2e-b974-bcbd5ed1ceb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160601" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & >0 \\\\ -4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png)"} :step/id #uuid "9221948f-2b37-566f-90fe-71f88ee2a512" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773f7224-683a-5657-93fd-0b49bba9ddc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389190} {:answer/id #uuid "3223453f-3db3-5cf7-9170-0776a21f7b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c24c3-b6bf-58dc-bb07-e1245dcf6b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389192} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae8e4b1-ec09-543b-b30d-54e69ad80dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/10/105e6e8a010b7b9001f75cf17e11cbbc1e031d83cbc2ddfafc3595c745106f3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389193} {:answer/id #uuid "682bc6d2-be17-5ccb-842c-4b844ea68ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/090bab0ac1c6356010d83a6ad54c59b53b7dd12062fb5e331a936ce3f3a1b411.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd81eba-4d1a-5e26-b6d5-47144737edcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99972da5-e4e5-5505-b6d1-ad2a467bcb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-161839" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"} :step/id #uuid "9363e01f-5717-561f-9373-b14a45d02277" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27184dd5-9306-5151-ba71-71c0184555b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275833} {:answer/id #uuid "921684d8-1a85-53da-b8a7-23bd356d4d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b6ecd9-7efa-5fbf-99f4-87cf3cf2fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276980} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4d180-9148-56f8-af89-c87393729376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277007} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ef6cf-c02f-531a-934f-84c5afac9290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276799} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f823688-c59b-5a64-9e51-9b7c368087e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b1f8f7b-fd82-5c84-a276-7fa2fd9b071a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160383" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot (-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c3f93cf7-3efb-5b9d-bfa9-7160f788cbe9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc0ac04-220f-5368-899c-f4b853d6caf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} {:answer/id #uuid "f515d3db-880a-5d23-99f0-9cb65e633ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369876} {:answer/id #uuid "57254211-1cc3-5a7f-87e1-0c345d6ba0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369877} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab4227a-a1c7-56de-9a4e-de04cc753871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369878} {:answer/id #uuid "47ca5fb3-37c5-5519-9b75-0484cfdf1808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} :answer-field/id #uuid "c478e331-2d1a-585a-9599-198d7061ae49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91c905cb-29ef-5cbc-a791-764e51ba7eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-160445" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fba89b3b-6b30-58fc-958c-99b3a9d49bb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406805}}] :activity/title "The Jacobian" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406803}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417359} :activity/id #uuid "5eeaa0e4-09d3-5d55-9a9f-ba1c84383049" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219 :topic/id #uuid "893b600f-8843-5755-aed4-951036b65cbd" :topic/math-academy-id 1999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e0e11f-ca64-573d-aa4a-39c0085748ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275562} {:answer/id #uuid "2571ec42-1410-5512-abb4-5d9f33e67cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275817} {:answer/id #uuid "6056f233-baaf-59fc-ab26-47501271c259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276339} {:answer/id #uuid "e97346e5-01ac-55ce-b840-edd902ee3c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517} {:answer/id #uuid "fa81b135-dfdc-52eb-85bc-1dd61c22d3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517} :answer-field/id #uuid "946d36e5-48fa-5671-b5c2-6b9df0953fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "876a5d15-9cbd-5150-8246-c71dbabaa45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160431" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "51873d91-9d02-596a-8782-ed5264ede6c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48838786-15c4-529e-9b61-9a4ae3631e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279238} {:answer/id #uuid "644450bf-23df-5be6-9017-bc23e84a13fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279504} {:answer/id #uuid "71de0d63-b9fe-5bf9-98a3-3aa0f0728bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279627} {:answer/id #uuid "743bb53f-15e9-5c76-9499-48070c482fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7f/7f214af0b5d71689ec7f21fff043e1779f1d3244c13fdbaf7b8df7f408d66298.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279641} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8384cd-3f08-52be-b19d-6807ce1004b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/14/14015cadab7d5dc8a9c33d0c1a57a44a79506bd01ba9b1cfe8ec1d280eb3fbb6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279504} :answer-field/id #uuid "158dba50-6c99-530b-a3b4-71c1f3ef60c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "613ff480-77bb-508e-adea-361b7545c613" :question/math-academy-id "q-161838" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2v)-(2u)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2v-2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-2u & <0 \\\\ 2v & <2u \\\\ v & <u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5be3b3f2-cb7d-5d95-8a36-731fef7d85d5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0d4b0b-8093-5290-b6b4-484e2a3c019b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417350} {:answer/id #uuid "3f52c558-a8f4-5f12-84e4-136a7b9663ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417351} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3faf64-35fa-5814-9c5b-2c546aef7f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417352} {:answer/id #uuid "c802b759-c3a3-5234-a525-52e3dcf0d733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417353} {:answer/id #uuid "df929076-a9d7-5c37-b534-2a48f5c0f2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417353} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6520f4c-cce8-5e56-adce-0289e4c3517a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2756cd36-f06f-563c-8e51-e0b858d03f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-160387" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ u+2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where\n$[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+2{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 4v-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=24(1)(1)-2=22.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8efc25d4-4fc0-5616-89ad-709e46dd706b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417357}}] :activity/title "The Jacobian" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417349}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455525} :activity/id #uuid "6a553618-5c66-5afb-86a1-1946f278abb1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219 :topic/id #uuid "893b600f-8843-5755-aed4-951036b65cbd" :topic/math-academy-id 1999} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3e495a-4d39-5ca6-b3be-a0813078a0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278868} {:answer/id #uuid "a23d32b3-d4d4-5e82-8301-ae2d3c9a6002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/61/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280033} {:answer/id #uuid "d5175981-dd9b-5a24-a6b2-37545e23ce11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9e/9ecab1b0f048e83b57d04a60c1cf20ee5ed0e61257577cf42c1a091976c04473.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280472} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9b7a18-0143-5fce-848a-c380d264cf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7f/7fc8ce1e1eaa3eb4e4acbbc167bdc4a479591bea21d2acdeaf799bd99d6b1150.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280544} {:answer/id #uuid "f5be8a96-29fb-524c-92e1-8f2da938314c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280033} :answer-field/id #uuid "262596a8-f5e0-5444-9e30-382a5a13609e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee68f9ff-69b4-5317-9406-24330023ed30" :question/math-academy-id "q-161855" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot (1)-(2u)\\cdot u{e}^{uv} \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & >0 \\\\ v & >2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/61/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png)"} :step/id #uuid "0f09f356-128b-5de9-9495-de9456b0bf64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48838786-15c4-529e-9b61-9a4ae3631e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279238} {:answer/id #uuid "644450bf-23df-5be6-9017-bc23e84a13fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279504} {:answer/id #uuid "7